The callback `onDisabledReportFieldsSwitchPress` is named for the event slot it is wired to (a disabled switch press) rather than the action it performs. Per STYLE.md, name the function for what it does; the `on*` naming belongs on the JSX prop, not on the declaration.

Rename it to describe the behavior, e.g.:

```tsx
const showCannotDisableImportedFieldsModal = () => {
    if (!hasImportedReportField) {
        return;
    }
    showConfirmModal({ /* ... */ });
};
// ...
disabledAction={withReadOnlyFallback(showCannotDisableImportedFieldsModal)}
```

The function is declared as `handleSignOutPress`, naming it after the event slot (`...Press`) rather than the action it performs. Per STYLE.md, a callback method should be named for what it does, not for the event it happens to be wired to. The `on*`/`handle*` naming belongs on the JSX prop, not on the function definition.

Rename the declaration to describe the behavior and keep the `on*` naming only at the call site:

```tsx
const confirmSignOut = async () => {
    const result = await showConfirmModal({ /* ... */ });
    if (result.action === ModalActions.CONFIRM) {
        signOutAndRedirectToSignIn();
    }
};

// ...
<Button onPress={confirmSignOut}>
```

The function is declared as `handleEnableTwoFactorAuthPress`, naming it after the event slot (`...Press`) rather than the action it performs. Per STYLE.md, a callback method should be named for what it does, not for the event it happens to be wired to. The `on*`/`handle*` naming belongs on the JSX prop, not on the function definition.

Rename the declaration to describe the behavior and keep the `on*` naming only at the call site:

```tsx
const enableTwoFactorAuth = () => {
    snapshotOnboardingResumePathIfNeeded();
    Navigation.navigate(getTwoFactorAuthRoute(ROUTES.SETTINGS_SECURITY, {forceSetup: true}));
};

// ...
<Button onPress={enableTwoFactorAuth}>
```

The newly declared handler `onToggleAutoUpdateGovernmentRate` is named for the event slot it is wired to (`on...`) rather than for the action it performs. Per `STYLE.md`, a callback's own declaration should be named for what it does; the `on*` naming belongs only on the JSX prop.

Rename the function to describe the action and keep the `on*` naming at the call site:

```tsx
const toggleAutoUpdateGovernmentRate = (isOn: boolean) => {
    if (!customUnit) {
        return;
    }
    setWorkspaceDistanceAutoUpdate(policyID, customUnit, isOn, governmentMileageRates ?? [], policy?.outputCurrency);
};

// ...
<Switch onToggle={toggleAutoUpdateGovernmentRate} />
```

`handleFilterHoverIn` is named after the event slot it is wired to (`onHoverIn`/`onFocus`) rather than what it does. The function actually exits describe mode and selects the hovered filter, so a behavior-based name is clearer and keeps the handler reusable. The `on*`/`handle*` convention belongs on the JSX prop, not the declaration.

```tsx
const selectFilter = (key: SearchFilter['key']) => {
    setIsDescribeMode(false);
    setSelectedFilter(key);
};
// ...
<FilterList onHoverIn={selectFilter} onFocus={selectFilter} />
```

`handleBulkPaySubItemSelected` is named for the event slot it is wired to (`onSubItemSelected`) rather than the action it performs. Per STYLE.md, a callback should be named for the behavior it triggers so it stays searchable by intent and reusable across triggers; the `on*`/`handle*` naming belongs on the JSX prop, not on the function definition.

Rename the declaration to describe the action, and keep the event name only at the call site. For example:

```tsx
const payBulkSelectedItem = (subItem: PopoverMenuItem, triggerKYCFlow: (kycParams: ContinueActionParams) => void) =>
    handleBulkPayItemSelected({ /* ... */ });

// call site keeps the on* naming on the prop:
onSubItemSelected={(subItem) => payBulkSelectedItem(subItem, triggerKYCFlow)}
```

The callback is declared as `onSave`, which names the event slot rather than the action it performs. Per STYLE.md, name the function for its behavior and keep the `on*` naming on the JSX prop.

```tsx
const saveVendor = (value?: string) => {
    updateDraftMerchantRule({vendorID: value});
};
// ...
<RuleSelectionBase.Picker onSave={saveVendor} ... />
```

`const handlePress = () => { ... }` is named for the event slot it is wired to rather than the action it performs. The function toggles selection of every item in the section (select-all / deselect-all). Name it for the behavior so it stays searchable by intent; keep the `on*`/`handle*` naming on the JSX prop only.

```tsx
const toggleSection = () => {
    if (isAllSelected) {
        // ...
    }
};

<Checkbox onPress={toggleSection} ... />
```

Per `contributingGuides/STYLE.md`, name a callback for the action it performs rather than the event slot it happens to fill. `handleSubmit` is named for the trigger (the submit button's `onSubmit`) instead of the behavior it runs (validating a selection and advancing to the next step). Naming by behavior keeps the handler searchable by intent and reusable across triggers.

Rename the function to describe what it does, e.g. `assignSelectedCardholder` (or `goToNextStep`), and keep `on*` on the JSX prop:

```tsx
const assignSelectedCardholder = () => {
    if (!selectedAssignee) {
        setShouldShowError(true);
        return;
    }
    submit(selectedAssignee);
};

// ...
<FormAlertWithSubmitButton onSubmit={assignSelectedCardholder} ... />
```

Per `contributingGuides/STYLE.md`, a callback should be named for the action it performs, not for the event slot it is wired to. `handleSelectRow` only tells the reader where it is attached (the `onSelectRow` slot); it does not describe what it does. The `on*`/`handle*` naming belongs on the JSX prop, not on the function definition.

Rename the function to describe the behavior, e.g. `selectAssignee`, and keep the event-slot name at the call site:

```tsx
const selectAssignee = (assignee: ListItem) => {
    setSelectedAssignee(assignee);
    setShouldShowError(false);
};

// ...
<SelectionList onSelectRow={selectAssignee} ... />
```

`handleOnSelectRow` is named for the event slot it forwards to rather than the action it performs. Per `STYLE.md`, name the callback for its behavior; the `handle*`/`on*` prefix belongs on the JSX prop, not the function definition.

Rename it to describe the action, e.g.:
```tsx
const selectRow = () => {
    onSelectRow(item);
};
// ...
<ExpenseReportListItemRowNarrow onCheckboxPress={selectRow} ... />
<RadioButton onPress={selectRow} ... />
```

`onSelection` is named for the event slot (`onSelectRow`) rather than the action it performs. Per `STYLE.md`, name the callback for its behavior; the `on*` prefix belongs on the JSX prop, not the function definition.

Rename it to describe the action, e.g.:
```tsx
const selectDestinationReport = (item: ListItem) => {
    setDestinationReportID(item.reportID);
};
// ...
<SelectionList onSelectRow={selectDestinationReport} ... />
```

`handleConfirm` is named for the event slot it is wired to (`onSubmit`) rather than the action it performs. Per `STYLE.md`, a callback should be named for what it does; the `handle*`/`on*` prefix belongs on the JSX prop, not the function definition.

Rename it to describe the action, e.g.:
```tsx
const mergeSelectedReports = () => {
    // ...
};
// ...
<FormAlertWithSubmitButton onSubmit={mergeSelectedReports} ... />
```

The handler `handleBackPress` is named for the triggering event (back press) rather than the action it performs (navigating back). Name it for the behavior and keep the `on*` prefix on the JSX prop only.

```tsx
const goBack = () => {
    Navigation.goBack(backPath);
};

// ...
<HeaderWithBackButton title={translate('workspace.common.planType')} onBackButtonPress={goBack} />
```

The handler `handleSelect` is named for the event slot it is wired to (`onSelectRow`) rather than for the action it performs. It saves the chosen plan type as a draft value and navigates back — its name should describe that behavior. The `on*`/`handle*` prefix belongs on the JSX prop, not the function definition.

```tsx
const savePlanType = (option: PlanTypeItem) => {
    setDraftValues(ONYXKEYS.FORMS.WORKSPACE_CONFIRMATION_FORM, {[INPUT_IDS.PLAN_TYPE]: option.value});
    Navigation.goBack(backPath, {shouldSkipFocusRestore: true});
};

// ...
<SelectionList onSelectRow={savePlanType} ... />
```
